    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH14-Skeletal/section-divergence-theorem.html">The Divergence Theorem</url></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!--
    The calculator, assets/grade-calculator.html (copied from the MATH 13
    one), is marked component="web": publication/publication.ptx keeps it
    and publication/publication-print.ptx drops it, so the PDF has the
    print paragraph pointing to the web page instead. It is a bare
    interactive, not a figure, so that the web page and the PDF number
    their figures and tables the same.
  -->
  <subsection xml:id="drop-lowest-midterm">
    <title>Grade Calculator</title>

    <p>
      Your lowest midterm exam score is dropped. Your best two midterms, out
      of 40 points, are scaled to the full 60 midterm points of
      <xref ref="table-components"/>, so the midterms still count for 60% of
      your course grade:
      <md>
        <mrow>\text{Midterm points} = \frac{\text{sum of your best two midterms}}{40}\times 60.</mrow>
      </md>
      The final exam (30 points) and the assignments (10 points) are
      unchanged, and your total out of 100 becomes a letter grade with the
      percentage ranges in <xref ref="table-letter-grades"/>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-grade-drop-example">
      <title>An example: from a C+ to a B+</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <col halign="right"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Component</cell>
          <cell>Score</cell>
          <cell>Without the drop</cell>
          <cell>With the drop</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Exam #1</cell><cell>8 / 20</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>dropped</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Exam #2</cell><cell>18 / 20</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>18</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Exam #3</cell><cell>18 / 20</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>18</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Midterm points (out of 60)</cell><cell/><cell><m>8 + 18 + 18 = 44</m></cell><cell><m>\frac{18 + 18}{40}\times 60 = 54</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Final Exam</cell><cell>25 / 30</cell><cell>25</cell><cell>25</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Assignments</cell><cell>9 / 10</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>9</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Total (out of 100)</cell><cell/><cell>78 (C+)</cell><cell>88 (B+)</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The best two midterms come to 36 out of 40, which is 90%, and that 90%
      counts for all 60 midterm points. The weak first exam simply stops
      counting.
    </p>

    <p component="web">
      Enter your scores in the calculator below to see your course grade with
      the lowest midterm dropped and any bonus points added. Bonus points are
      added to your best two midterms after the lowest one is dropped, so they
      count no matter which midterm is dropped, and your midterm total cannot
      go above 40 out of 40. Leave a box empty for an exam you have not
      taken yet: empty boxes count as 0, so the total shown is the lowest the
      scores you entered can give, and your letter grade appears once the
      three midterms, the final, and the assignments are entered (the bonus
      points box is optional). While the final is still to come, the
      calculator lists the final exam score each letter grade needs.
    </p>

    <interactive component="web" xml:id="interactive-grade-calculator"
                 iframe="grade-calculator.html"
                 width="100%" aspect="15:19"/>

    <p component="print">
      The web version of this syllabus has a grade calculator in this
      subsection: enter your scores and it shows your course grade with the
      lowest midterm dropped. It is at
      <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH14-Syllabus-Fall2026/math14-syllabus.html#drop-lowest-midterm" visual="mahmadi-ops.github.io/MATH14-Syllabus-Fall2026"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="letter-grades">
    <title>Letter Grades</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-grading">
      <caption>Your course grade comes from three exams, the final exam, and
      ten written assignments.</caption>
      <image source="grading.png" width="84%">
        <shortdescription>An instructor at a desk grading papers, holding up one marked with a circled A</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A cartoon of an instructor sitting at a desk, smiling and holding
            up a graded paper with a large letter A circled in red. A tall
            stack of assignments with colored sticky-note tabs sits on the left
            of the desk, and two more papers marked with a circled A lie in
            front of him. He has a red pen in his other hand, and there is a
            mug of pens, a stack of books, and a potted plant on the desk.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Your total out of 100 points is converted to a letter grade using the
      percentage ranges in <xref ref="table-letter-grades"/>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-letter-grades">
      <title>Percentage ranges and letter grades</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Letter grade</cell>
          <cell>Percentage range</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>A</cell><cell>94 <ndash/> 100</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A<ndash/></cell><cell>90 <ndash/> 93</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B+</cell><cell>87 <ndash/> 89</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B</cell><cell>83 <ndash/> 86</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B<ndash/></cell><cell>80 <ndash/> 82</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C+</cell><cell>77 <ndash/> 79</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C</cell><cell>73 <ndash/> 76</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C<ndash/></cell><cell>70 <ndash/> 72</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D+</cell><cell>67 <ndash/> 69</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D</cell><cell>63 <ndash/> 66</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D<ndash/></cell><cell>60 <ndash/> 62</cell></row>
        <row><cell>F</cell><cell>59 or lower</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>
</section>
